# drivers/storage/conf: the phone's flash, and the block devices of the images the port boots from.
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_<NAME>=y in a defconfig builds a driver into the kernel; "standard" files are always built.
# tools/hakios-config.py reads this file; docs/drivers-config.md says what each word means and how it reaches XNU.

standard bsd/dev/hakios.image.c
	help    a file of one of the RAM disks by path, for the drivers that load firmware before there is a root

standard bsd/dev/hakios.sparse.c
	help    /dev/rsp0, a block device over an image that holds only some of its blocks (apfs_sparse=)

standard bsd/dev/hakios.apfs_check.c apfs
	help    a walk of the APFS volume on /dev/rvblk0 through VFS, to compare with tools/apfs/apfs.py

driver UFS
	file    bsd/dev/hakios.ufs.c
	file    bsd/dev/hakios.ufshc.c
	file    bsd/dev/hakios.ext4.c
	help    the phone's UFS flash: a block device for each GPT partition (ufs=1, ufs=2), and the UFS host controller
	help    driven from the kernel (ufs_native=): beside UEFI's (1, the POCO), or its own for the run (4; QEMU's -device
	help    ufs, the S9's with ufs_native_soc=exynos9810 as UEFI left it, never reset: on the phone through ufs=3), in steps (ufs_native_step=1
	help    look, 2 the link, 3 NOP OUT, 4 READ CAPACITY, 5 the GPTs; the S9's default 1). THE ONLY CODE OF THE KERNEL THAT MAY WRITE TO THE
	help    PHONE (ufs_write=1, the partition `darwin` only; ufs_native_write=, one partition, never EFS, BOOT, PARAM ...):
	help    see tools/provenance.allow. ufs=3 (the S9's default, seen working on the phone): the loader serves as with ufs=2, and the
	help    kernel's own driver takes the controller after a self-test against the loader's reads (LBA 0, the GPTs, three
	help    blocks of every partition), falling back to ufs=2 on any difference; every write fenced in the whitelist itself
	help    (USERDATA only, with ufs_write=1; never EFS, RADIO, BOOT, the GPTs, LBA 0-5, a boot unit); ufs_native_ro=1 all read only.
	help    And a file of one of its ext4 partitions, read only (hakios.ext4.c: the S9's
	help    VENDOR, where its Wi-Fi and Bluetooth firmware is, read at run time; tools/ext4-test.py)

driver VIRTIO_BLK
	file    bsd/dev/hakios.virtio_blk.c
	help    a disk image of the host as /dev/rvblk0 (virtio_blk=1). QEMU only
